From: Canaan K230 BSP <bsp@canaan-creative.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] media: canaan: vpu: allocate the MMU page array with kvzalloc

On a 128 MB board the encoder fails to start perhaps one boot in three,
and once it has failed it stays failed: every DQBUF returns EIO until
the process is restarted. The kernel log says

  smart_ipc: page allocation failure: order:6, mode:0xcc0(GFP_KERNEL)
   mvx_mmu_alloc_pages+0x4e/0x1a4
  MVX if: Could not find pages for RPC resize. va=0x20000000.

order-6 is 256 kB of physically contiguous memory, and there is plenty
of memory free at the time - the Mem-Info dump shows free:3676 pages.
It is not an out-of-memory condition. free_cma:2482 of those pages are
CMA, and every free block of order 5 or above in the buddy lists is
tagged (C), i.e. MIGRATE_CMA only. GFP_KERNEL is unmovable and must
not take CMA pages, so the largest block this allocation can actually
have is 64 kB. Compaction cannot build a bigger one either: of the
~52 MB outside the CMA area, ~27 MB is slab and page tables, which are
unmovable and spread across most of the 2 MB pageblocks. A single
unmovable page inside a 256 kB window is enough to rule that window
out forever. That is why it is probabilistic and why a reboot fixes it.

What is being allocated is not pixel data. mvx_mmu_alloc_pages() sizes
its trailing phys_addr_t pages[] array from the capacity argument, and
on the RPC mem_alloc path in mvx_firmware_v2.c that capacity is the
firmware's declared max_size for the region, not the size actually
requested. A region whose max_size is 128 MB gives 32768 entries,
8 bytes each: 256 kB of index table for a mapping that at that moment
holds a couple of hundred pages. The frames themselves come from CMA
through mvx_mmu_alloc_contiguous_pages(), and that call succeeds.

The array has no reason to be physically contiguous. Every use of
pages->pages[i] in the driver reads one element at a time from the CPU,
to feed map_page() into the MVE page table, to free the page again, or
to dma_sync it. The array is never itself handed to the DMA API and
never passed through virt_to_phys() as a block. kvzalloc() is therefore
safe, and above one page it falls back to vmalloc, which does not care
how fragmented the buddy lists are.

Convert both constructors and both free paths. The side effect is that
the allocation is no longer tied to the device's devres list, so it is
not reclaimed automatically if the device is unbound with pages still
outstanding; mvx_mmu_free_pages() is the only owner and is called from
session teardown, so that path is unchanged in practice.

The mvx_mmu_dma_buf allocations stay on devm_kzalloc. They are a few
dozen bytes each and have never been the problem.

Signed-off-by: Canaan K230 BSP <bsp@canaan-creative.com>
--- a/drivers/media/platform/canaan/vpu/mvx_mmu.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/canaan/vpu/mvx_mmu.c
@@ -36,7 +36,9 @@
 #include <linux/dma-map-ops.h>
 #include <linux/gfp.h>
 #include <linux/list.h>
+#include <linux/mm.h>
 #include <linux/sched.h>
+#include <linux/slab.h>
 #include "mvx_mmu.h"
 #include "mvx_log_group.h"
 
@@ -708,9 +710,18 @@
 	capacity = roundup(capacity, MVX_PAGES_PER_PAGE);
 	capacity = max(count, capacity);
 
-	pages = devm_kzalloc(dev,
-			     sizeof(*pages) + sizeof(phys_addr_t) * capacity,
-			     GFP_KERNEL);
+	/*
+	 * capacity comes from the firmware's declared max_size for the
+	 * region, not from what is actually in use, so this array is sized
+	 * for a ceiling the board will never reach. kmalloc would ask the
+	 * buddy allocator for one contiguous block of that size - 256 kB,
+	 * order-6, for a region whose max_size is 128 MB - and after a few
+	 * minutes of uptime there is no unmovable block that large left.
+	 * The array is only ever read by the CPU to feed map_page(), so it
+	 * does not have to be physically contiguous. Let it be vmalloc'd.
+	 */
+	pages = kvzalloc(sizeof(*pages) + sizeof(phys_addr_t) * capacity,
+			 GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (pages == NULL)
 		return ERR_PTR(-ENOMEM);
 
@@ -766,9 +777,8 @@
 	capacity = roundup(capacity, MVX_PAGES_PER_PAGE);
 	capacity = max(count, capacity);
 
-	pages = devm_kzalloc(dev,
-			     sizeof(*pages) + sizeof(phys_addr_t) * capacity,
-			     GFP_KERNEL);
+	pages = kvzalloc(sizeof(*pages) + sizeof(phys_addr_t) * capacity,
+			 GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (pages == NULL)
 		return ERR_PTR(-ENOMEM);
 
@@ -780,7 +790,7 @@
 
 	ret = append_sg_table(pages, sgt);
 	if (ret != 0) {
-		devm_kfree(dev, pages);
+		kvfree(pages);
 		return ERR_PTR(ret);
 	}
 
@@ -959,7 +969,7 @@
 		devm_kfree(pages->dev, mbuf);
 	}
 
-	devm_kfree(pages->dev, pages);
+	kvfree(pages);
 }
 
 size_t mvx_mmu_size_pages(struct mvx_mmu_pages *pages)
